          "Previous pregnancy complications can change monitoring discussions, so discharge summaries and operative notes may be more useful than repeating broad screening. Advice should also fit the person's life, access, culture, work and caregiving demands. A technically ideal plan that cannot be followed is not genuinely individual care. The final reviewed version should therefore offer options, state uncertainty plainly and avoid implying that effort, body size or perfect adherence determines the outcome.",
          "The partner’s health, medicines and fertility history also matter where sperm is involved; preconception care should not place the entire burden on one person. The reader can prepare by recording a few representative examples, including what happened immediately before the symptom and how long it affected ordinary activity. There is no need to provoke the symptom for the diary. The record is useful only if it reduces guesswork and supports shared decisions; it is not a score, clearance tool or substitute for examination.",
