    bodyFingerprintText:
      "What is diastasis recti, and when should I get it checked?\nDiastasis recti is a widening and change in the connective tissue between the two rectus abdominal muscles. It is common during and after pregnancy, but appearance or finger width alone does not describe function. An assessment considers tension, movement, symptoms and your goals, while a painful or unexplained bulge may need medical review.\nWhat abdominal separation means\nThe rectus muscles run vertically at the front of the abdomen. Between them is connective tissue called the linea alba. During pregnancy this tissue adapts as the abdomen expands. A continued widening after birth is often called diastasis recti, but it is not a tear and it does not mean the muscles have split into two disconnected parts.\nPeople may notice doming during effort, a softer midline, difficulty managing load or no symptoms at all. A similar-looking bulge can have another explanation. Photographs, body shape and one measurement cannot settle the diagnosis or decide what treatment is needed.\nThe linea alba is connective tissue designed to adapt under load, so describing it as broken or torn can create fear that the anatomy does not support. For what abdominal separation means, Dr. Zalak Shah (PT) would place this detail beside the timing, associated symptoms and the reader's own goal. It should inform a conversation, not become a home diagnostic rule. If the detail changes suddenly, is severe or appears with a red-flag symptom, the safer next step is medical assessment rather than repeatedly testing or treating it alone.\nTissue tension and the person’s ability to transfer force during daily tasks may be more informative than appearance in a relaxed photograph. Advice should also fit the person's life, access, culture, work and caregiving demands. A technically ideal plan that cannot be followed is not genuinely individual care. The final reviewed version should therefore offer options, state uncertainty plainly and avoid implying that effort, body size or perfect adherence determines the outcome.\nA focal lump that behaves differently from the midline raises a separate medical question, particularly when pain, vomiting or non-reducibility is present. The reader can prepare by recording a few representative examples, including what happened immediately before the symptom and how long it affected ordinary activity. There is no need to provoke the symptom for the diary. The record is useful only if it reduces guesswork and supports shared decisions; it is not a score, clearance tool or substitute for examination.\nWhat a home observation can and cannot tell you\nA gentle self-observation may help you notice where the midline changes during a familiar movement. Finger width varies with hand size, position, effort and technique, so it should not become a pass-fail score. Repeated hard testing can add worry without improving the information.\nAny demonstration should be created and checked by a clinician. If you are pregnant, newly postnatal, recovering from surgery or have pain, ask before following a video. The separate guide on checking for diastasis explains these limits in more detail.\nPregnancy is a common context for diastasis, but abdominal separation can also be discussed outside pregnancy and after different abdominal histories. This matters because two people asking “What is diastasis recti?” may need different next steps even when one surface detail sounds similar. A review should establish what began first, what has changed, which treatments or adaptations have already been tried, and what would count as meaningful improvement. That is more clinically useful than promising one standard programme or deadline.\nPelvic-floor symptoms, back or pelvic pain and fear of movement can coexist and should be assessed rather than attributed automatically to the gap. For what a home observation can and cannot tell you, Dr. Zalak Shah (PT) would place this detail beside the timing, associated symptoms and the reader's own goal. It should inform a conversation, not become a home diagnostic rule. If the detail changes suddenly, is severe or appears with a red-flag symptom, the safer next step is medical assessment rather than repeatedly testing or treating it alone.\nWhat physiotherapy assesses\nDr. Zalak Shah (PT) may look at breathing, abdominal-wall tension, pressure management, pelvic-floor symptoms and how your trunk responds to tasks you value. The plan may involve graded strength and movement changes, but it should not promise to close a gap or flatten the abdomen.\nFunction matters: getting out of bed, lifting, exercise confidence and symptom control may be more meaningful than a single distance. The assessment should also identify when a finding sits outside physiotherapy and needs a medical opinion.\nDoming is an observation about how pressure is managed during a task, not a moral sign that the movement was done badly. In clinic, the practical question is what this changes: whether observation is enough, whether examination or a selected test could add information, and which professional should lead care. Dr. Zalak Shah (PT) should explain the limits of the assessment as clearly as its findings. Routine booking remains separate from urgent local care for severe or rapidly worsening symptoms.\nRehabilitation can use graded loading; banning all abdominal work may reduce confidence and capacity without addressing the individual problem. This matters because two people asking “What is diastasis recti?” may need different next steps even when one surface detail sounds similar. A review should establish what began first, what has changed, which treatments or adaptations have already been tried, and what would count as meaningful improvement. That is more clinically useful than promising one standard programme or deadline.\nWhen medical assessment is needed\nSeek routine assessment if you have persistent pain, uncertainty about a bulge, pelvic-floor symptoms or difficulty returning to daily activity. A new lump that is focal, painful, firm, cannot be gently reduced, or is associated with vomiting or feeling acutely unwell requires prompt medical assessment because a hernia or another condition may need exclusion.\nSevere sudden abdominal pain, repeated vomiting or collapse needs urgent local care. Do not send an emergency photograph over WhatsApp and wait for a response.\nGap width changes with position, breathing and effort, which is why one home measurement should not become a permanent severity label. The reader can prepare by recording a few representative examples, including what happened immediately before the symptom and how long it affected ordinary activity. There is no need to provoke the symptom for the diary. The record is useful only if it reduces guesswork and supports shared decisions; it is not a score, clearance tool or substitute for examination.\nBody-image concerns deserve respectful discussion, but physiotherapy should not promise a flat abdomen or use before-and-after photographs as clinical proof. In clinic, the practical question is what this changes: whether observation is enough, whether examination or a selected test could add information, and which professional should lead care. Dr. Zalak Shah (PT) should explain the limits of the assessment as clearly as its findings. Routine booking remains separate from urgent local care for severe or rapidly worsening symptoms.",
