          "A useful way to think about “Which symptoms should I pay attention to” is to separate observation from interpretation. Write down what happened, when it began, what makes it better or worse, and what it prevents you from doing. Include relevant cycle, pregnancy, birth, treatment or medical context, but avoid deciding the diagnosis before the appointment. Dr. Zalak Shah (PT) can then place that pattern alongside examination or test findings when those are indicated. This makes the consultation more precise and reduces the chance that one striking symptom, one scan line or one social-media explanation dominates the whole discussion. This point relates specifically to how do i know if my pelvic floor needs an assessment? and the questions covered in section 1; it should be checked against the clinician’s final review before publication.",
          "The next decision should be proportionate to the problem. A stable symptom may allow time for a planned review, a diary and shared discussion; a sudden or severe change may require same-day or emergency assessment. Online education cannot examine you, measure blood loss, assess hydration, confirm pregnancy location or test muscle and nerve function. If the warning signs already listed in this article are present, use local urgent services. For routine care, ask what the clinician is trying to clarify, what alternatives remain possible and what information would genuinely change the plan. This point relates specifically to how do i know if my pelvic floor needs an assessment? and the questions covered in section 1; it should be checked against the clinician’s final review before publication.",
